  "text_md": "nowhere dense sets), which are classically equivalent to each other. However, the combinatorial definition of κ-meagre sets coincides with the topological notion if and only if κ is countable or inaccessible, as was shown in [BHZ, §4]. Even though the ideals are distinct if κ is uncountable but not inaccessible, it is an open question whether the cardinal invariants given by the combinatorial κ-meagre ideal are consistently distinct from those given by the topological κ-meagre ideal:\n\nQuestion 2.3. Let the combinatorially κ-meagre ideal M comb κbe defined as in [BHZ, Def. 4.4]. Is\n\n|||κ|\n|---|---|---|\n|comb κ|comb κ||\n\nnon(M) < non(Mκ) or cov(Mκ) < cov(M) consistent for κ that are not inaccessible?\n\nThe characterisation of cof(N) and add(N) from Theorem 2.1 cannot be generalised to the higher context, not just because we would need a higher analogue of N, but also because the cardinality of d h κ(∈ ∗ ) is highly dependent on the choice of h ∈ κ κ when κ is inaccessible, as we will discuss in Section 3.2. For successor κ, Theorem 2.1 looks very different:\n\n|||κ ∞|h κ ∗|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|h κ ∗|κ ∞|||\n\nTheorem 2.4 ([Hyt06], [MS, Thm. 4.6]). If κ is a regular successor, then b (✟=✟) = b (∈) = bκ and dκ= d (∈) ≥ d (✟=✟). If we also assume κ <κ = κ, then the last inequality becomes an equality.\n\nQuestion 2.5 ([MS, §4]). Is dκ(✟=✟ ∞ ) < dκconsistent for κ a successor cardinal and κ <κ > κ?\n\nIt was observed by Truss and Miller that the cardinal characteristics add(M) and cof(M) can be expressed in terms of the other cardinals of the Cicho´n diagram.\n\nTheorem 2.6 ([Tru77], [Mil81]). add(M) = min {b, cov(M)} and cof(M) = max {d, non(M)}.\n\nThe same characterisation generalises to the higher Cicho´n diagram if we make strong enough assumptions on κ. We note that this is not provable for all uncountable κ. For example, Brendle [Bre22] showed that κ <κ < cof(Mκ), and thus max {dκ, non(Mκ)} < cof(Mκ) is consistent, and holds whenever max {dκ, non(Mκ)} ≤ κ <κ.\n\nTheorem 2.7 ([Bre22]). min {bκ, cov(Mκ) ≥ max {dκ, non(Mκ)}.\n\n|add(M|) =|)} and cof(M|\n|---|---|---|\n|<κ|κ|κ|\n\nUnder assumption of κ = κ the inequality becomes an equality.\n\nAs for the other eight cardinal characteristics of the classical Cicho´n diagram, they appear to be as independent as is possible in the following sense: any assignment of the cardinalities ℵ₁ and ℵ₂ to the cardinals of the classical Cicho´n diagram compatible with both Theorem 2.6 and the arrows in the diagram, is consistent (see [BJ95, Sections 7.5 and 7.6] for each of these cases). This implies that the classical Cicho´n diagram is complete, as no arrows are missing from the diagram. In fact, Goldstern, Kellner, Mej´ıa, and Shelah [GKMS21; GKS19] showed that all eight cardinal characteristics can have mutually different cardinalities within the same model.\n",
